{
  "dom_id": "question-184770",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Let $n$ be an integer. Prove that ${n}^{3}$ is even implies $n$ is even.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⇒\\,{{field-2}}\n$$\n\nWe will prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nIf $n$ is {{field-3}}, then $n=$ {{field-4}} for some integer $a.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3} & ={{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-6}}. Since $a$ is {{field-7}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-8}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3} & ={{field-9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich {{field-10}} a multiple of $2.$ Therefore ${n}^{3}$ is {{field-11}}.",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
